---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import { getGuide } from "../../data/guides";

const guide = getGuide("security-testing");
---

<DocsLayout guide={guide}>
  <section class="docs-hero docs-searchable" id="overview">
    <p class="docs-kicker">Security Testing</p>
    <h1>
      Test the scanner.<span>Keep the gaps visible.</span>
    </h1>
    <p>
      Repyy processes hostile input. Tests exercise the scanner itself; no fixture is intentionally executed as an assignment. Passing tests do not establish
      that a repository or Repyy is free of vulnerabilities.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="docs-section docs-searchable" id="existing-regression-coverage">
    <h2>Behavioral test coverage</h2>
    <p>
      Unit tests cover rule matching, severity/context, configuration and suppressions, report fields and exit policy. Integration tests invoke the built CLI
      against inert local fixtures. Source tests verify URL rejection and sanitized Git configuration. Docker unit tests use a fake Docker executable to test
      invocation and hostile JSON handling; they are not live isolation-escape tests. CI separately builds and starts the worker image for a version smoke
      check.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="https://github.com/Kevin-Umali/repyy/blob/main/internal/scan/repository_test.go">
        <code>repository_test.go</code>
      </a>{" "}
      covers Git metadata and symlinks;
      <a href="https://github.com/Kevin-Umali/repyy/blob/main/internal/scan/archive_test.go">
        <code>archive_test.go</code>
      </a>{" "}
      covers archive traversal and nested inspection;
      <a href="https://github.com/Kevin-Umali/repyy/blob/main/internal/scan/active_content_test.go">
        <code>active_content_test.go</code>
      </a>{" "}
      covers staged execution, active document content, and VSIX packages;
      <a href="https://github.com/Kevin-Umali/repyy/blob/main/internal/scan/context_test.go">
        <code>context_test.go</code>
      </a>{" "}
      checks prompt-injection context; and
      <a href="https://github.com/Kevin-Umali/repyy/blob/main/internal/scan/scanner_test.go">
        <code>scanner_test.go</code>
      </a>{" "}
      exercises cross-surface acceptance.
      <code>internal/output/output_test.go</code> checks terminal control neutralization, HTML escaping, source-link validation, redaction and report integrity.{" "}
      <code>internal/intel/update_test.go</code> covers signed updates and failure behavior.
    </p>
    <p>
      Inert fixtures pair expected findings with benign controls, including decoded content, startup hooks, remote-response execution, and package-version
      indicators.
    </p>
    <p>
      Tests use fixed inert examples with expected findings and benign controls. A test that derives its expected match from the production regex can pass when
      the regex changes incorrectly. Catalog ID lists and snapshots can catch edits but do not prove detection. New bug tests must cover a behavior that
      existing tests miss; expand an existing test when it already covers that path.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="docs-section docs-searchable" id="fuzz-targets-and-properties">
    <h2>Fuzz targets and properties</h2>
    <div class="docs-table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Evidence table" tabindex="0">
      <table class="docs-data-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Target</th>
            <th>Input surface</th>
            <th>Property / bounds</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>FuzzArchiveInspection</td>
            <td>ZIP, TAR and gzip bytes</td>
            <td>No crash or unbounded diagnostics; 64 KiB input, 32 entries, 64 KiB expansion, 16 KiB file, depth 2, context deadline</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>FuzzArchivePaths</td>
            <td>Portable archive paths</td>
            <td>Accepted paths cannot normalize outside the root; 4 KiB input; Windows and POSIX seeds</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>FuzzSymlinkConfinement</td>
            <td>Symbolic-link targets</td>
            <td>Scanner reads no bytes through repository symlinks; 4 KiB target, controlled external sentinel</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>FuzzGitMetadata</td>
            <td>
              <code>.git/config</code>
            </td>
            <td>Metadata includes are data and cannot add external file bytes; 8 KiB input in an isolated temp root</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>FuzzConfiguration</td>
            <td>Explicit trusted YAML</td>
            <td>Parse/validation handles malformed input and rejects unsupported versions; 8 KiB input</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>FuzzManifests</td>
            <td>Eight manifest formats</td>
            <td>No panic and no negative source locations; 8 KiB input; no package-manager execution</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>FuzzHumanOutput</td>
            <td>Repository-controlled filenames/messages</td>
            <td>Terminal controls neutralized and injected script markup escaped in HTML; 4 KiB string</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>FuzzJSONReports</td>
            <td>Serialized report objects</td>
            <td>Accepted input re-renders as valid JSON; 16 KiB input</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Archive seeds include valid ZIP, TAR and TAR.GZ files, nested ZIPs, traversal paths, expansion limits and entry-count limits, alongside malformed inputs.
      These are bounded starting targets, not exhaustive coverage. Context cancellation is cooperative, not a hard CPU/memory guarantee. CI supplies a 30-minute
      package-job timeout and two workers per target. Concurrent symlink replacement, full Git transport/SSH behavior, live Docker isolation and every report
      field still need broader testing and independent review.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="docs-section docs-searchable" id="run-and-reproduce">
    <h2>Run and reproduce</h2>
    <pre class="docs-example"><code>make check
make security
go test ./internal/scan -run '^$' -fuzz '^FuzzArchiveInspection$' -fuzztime 50000x -parallel 2
</code></pre>
    <p>
      Use the corresponding package and target name for the remaining targets. Ordinary <code>go test</code> runs the seed corpus. CI uses fixed execution
      counts per target on relevant changes and all main pushes. The daily Security evidence workflow runs ten times the CI counts. The
      <a href="https://github.com/Kevin-Umali/repyy/blob/main/.github/workflows/fuzz.yml">fuzz workflow</a> defines the budgets. Fixed execution budgets avoid a
      possible Go fuzz-coordinator duration deadline race. Archive limits remain unchanged. Read the workflow run rather than assuming a scheduled run happened.
      Fuzz logs and available failing inputs are uploaded even if a target fails.
    </p>
    <p>
      For a failure, preserve the generated corpus input under the package's <code>testdata/fuzz/TARGET/</code> and minimize it. Fix the violated property and
      rerun both the seed and fuzz target. Add a named behavioral test when existing coverage cannot express that property; otherwise expand the relevant test.
      Do not replace the input with live malware or remove it to obtain a passing run.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="docs-section docs-searchable" id="public-benchmark">
    <h2>Public benchmark</h2>
    <p>
      The <a href="../demo/sample/results.json">inert sample benchmark</a> and its versioned paired controls are a separate behavioral check. Per-release
      results are attached by the release workflow. Known misses and all observed rules remain public; a synthetic benchmark does not establish general
      accuracy.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="docs-section docs-searchable" id="known-gaps-and-independent-review">
    <h2>Known gaps and independent review</h2>
    <p>
      Short fuzz runs cannot exhaust the input space. Tests using fake host tools cannot prove the behavior of every installed Git/Docker version. OS-specific
      permission and symlink behavior requires platform CI. Resource limits do not provide dedicated-VM isolation. Best-effort cleanup can leave temporary data
      after failures.
    </p>
    <p>
      Report newly found vulnerabilities using <a href="https://github.com/Kevin-Umali/repyy/security/advisories/new">private reporting</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="docs-section docs-searchable" id="independent-review" data-search="external independent security audit review scope report retest">
    <h2>Independent review brief</h2>
    <p>The brief is ready for review. An independent review is pending, and the project makes no independent audit claim.</p>
    <h3>Prepare the evidence</h3>
    <p>
      Give the reviewer a fixed release tag and full commit, the public trust claims, successful release verification, inert demo and benchmark results, fuzz
      evidence, and known issue status.
    </p>
    <h3>Review scope</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Threat-model accuracy, target-code non-execution, and host Git and SSH retrieval and credentials.</li>
      <li>Temporary-directory and container cleanup, archive and symlink confinement, Windows and Unix file handling, and Docker fetch and scan boundaries.</li>
      <li>Terminal and HTML escaping, source links, secret redaction, and signed intelligence update and rollback.</li>
      <li>Release identity, signing, SBOM scope, attestations, and publication ordering.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Use inert inputs. Keep private assignments, active malware, real credentials, target execution, general detection completeness, and hosted private
      scanning outside the review scope.
    </p>
    <h3>Choose a reviewer and publish the results</h3>
    <p>
      Look for public work on Go parser security, filesystem confinement, Git and SSH security, container boundaries, or supply-chain verification. Ask about
      relevant past work, conflicts, availability, methods, and publication terms. One starting point is the
      <a href="https://ostif.org/get-an-audit/">OSTIF audit request channel</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Publish the reviewer's identity, conflicts, exact release and commit, dates, methods, scope, and limitations. For each finding, record the severity,
      affected behavior, inert reproduction, maintainer response, fix commit, and retest outcome. Keep unresolved findings in the published record.
    </p>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
